menu "RuuviTag frequency analyzer"

# There are deliberately no signal-processing options here.
#
# Transform length, overlap, window function and reference level all live on the
# host, where changing one costs a page reload rather than a reflash (ADR-0003).
# A tuning knob in this file would be a number somebody has to unscrew the tag
# from a wall to change. The only things left are how the board behaves as a
# board - which includes what it costs to run.

config RFA_IDLE_PERIOD_S
	int "Seconds between broadcast spectra when idle"
	default 60
	range 5 3600
	help
	  How often an idle tag wakes the accelerometer to 400 Hz, takes one
	  transform window and queues a 0xC2 spectrum.

	  This is the main battery lever. A burst is ~3 s, so 60 s is roughly a
	  5% duty cycle on the sensor; 10 s would be ~30% and several times the
	  idle draw. It has no effect on a connected capture, which streams
	  continuously either way.

config RFA_WAKE_ON_MOTION
	bool "Burst on the accelerometer's activity interrupt"
	default y
	help
	  Sample when something actually moves, on top of the heartbeat. Worth
	  having on intermittent machinery, where it turns a fixed duty cycle
	  into one that follows the equipment. On something that vibrates
	  continuously it fires constantly and saves nothing - turn it off there
	  and rely on the heartbeat.

config RFA_MOTION_THRESHOLD_MG
	int "Activity threshold (mg)"
	default 256
	range 16 2000
	depends on RFA_WAKE_ON_MOTION
	help
	  Quantised to 16 mg steps at the +/-2 g full scale, and applied after a
	  high-pass filter, so it is a threshold on change rather than on gravity.

	  Note the sensor idles in 8-bit low-power mode, where one LSB is 16 mg -
	  so this is a threshold on a coarse signal, not on the 1 mg resolution
	  the analyser sees during a burst.

	  64 mg was the first guess and it was wrong: on a bench with a steady
	  72 mg component at 44.5 Hz the tag retriggered within ~230 ms of every
	  burst and spent 92% of its life awake. 256 mg clears that. It is still
	  site-specific - use command 0x02 on the Control characteristic to tune
	  it live, watch DF5's movement counter, then set the value you found
	  here.

config RFA_MOTION_DURATION
	int "Samples above threshold before the interrupt fires"
	default 4
	range 1 127
	depends on RFA_WAKE_ON_MOTION
	help
	  The sensor idles at 10 Hz, so 4 samples is ~400 ms of sustained motion
	  before a burst. Raising this rejects knocks and footsteps at the cost of
	  latency; lowering it makes the tag jumpier.

config RFA_ADV_INTERVAL_MS
	int "Advertising interval (ms)"
	default 1000
	range 100 10000
	help
	  Longer is cheaper, but discovery is what sets the floor in practice, not
	  the broadcast spectrum. At the edge of range a scanner only hears a
	  fraction of advertisements, and halving the interval halves the time to
	  find the tag - which at 2000 ms on a weak link was long enough to look
	  like a tag that was not there.

	  A 0xC2 frame takes 8 advertisements, so at 1000 ms one spectrum needs
	  ~8 s to transmit and still fits comfortably inside a 60 s idle period.

config RFA_STATUS_LEDS
	bool "Status LEDs"
	default y
	help
	  Green flashes per advertisement, red solid while a host is streaming.

	  On by default, which is not the cheapest choice and is deliberate. A
	  tag on a battery in another room, with the LEDs off and out of radio
	  range, gives you nothing at all to look at - there is no way to tell a
	  flat cell from a crash from a wall in the way. The flash costs roughly
	  15-20 uA and buys "walk up to it and see that it is alive".

	  Turn it off once a deployment is proven and you trust the link.

config RFA_LED_BOOT_MS
	int "Boot indication (ms)"
	default 200
	depends on RFA_STATUS_LEDS

config RFA_LED_FLASH_MS
	int "Advertisement flash (ms)"
	default 10
	depends on RFA_STATUS_LEDS
	help
	  Short enough to be cheap, long enough to see. Below about 5 ms the
	  flash is hard to catch by eye.

endmenu

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
